@vsm 1.0
// sensor_omega_integration/spec_2026-08-02.vsm
// Author: Claude [O] + rmw3 (S5), 2026-08-02.
// Grew from a direct question ("por que tampoco vimos eso, ni hubieron reportes
// algedonicos?") after the vsf-s4-clima-bridge crash-loop finding (22h+ silent,
// see CLIMA-BRIDGE-NATS-CRASHLOOP-FIXED in TASKS.vsm). rmw3's framing, verbatim,
// is the spec's own thesis and should not be paraphrased away in any future edit:
//
//   "tenemos que garantizar que este sistema se puede gobernar de manera
//   autonoma y esto no pasa por que cada decision de ajuste o revision pase
//   por el humano... el humano en el loop esta bien para las politicas, pero
//   no puedo estar supervisando cada organo del sistema... para eso tenemos
//   sensores y estos tienen que conectar... y para esto hemos mapeado un
//   espacio de R^128, para poder gobernar esa geometria compleja."
//
//   "para mi lo mas grave no es lo puntual, es lo general, que falten
//   parametros conectados a R^128."
//
// Status: formal spec, per rmw3 explicit request ("pasemoslo como spec formal.
// y luego el plan de implementacion"). Root-cause investigation for the
// triggering incident is COMPLETE (section 1) -- verified against real logs,
// not guessed. The governance framework (section 2) and implementation plan
// (section 3) are the deliverable rmw3 asked for.

spec(SENSOR-OMEGA-INTEGRATION) {

  relationship_to_other_docs: "This spec does NOT replace or duplicate
      docs/spec/monitoring_instruments.vsm (the canonical instrument
      catalogue -- single source of truth per feedback_single_source).
      monitoring_instruments.vsm gets a findings update (section 1) as part
      of this same work. This spec is the INCIDENT STUDY + the governance
      question that incident exposes -- same relationship as
      docs/spec/thermal_resource_governance/amendment_2026-08-02.vsm has to
      covenant_ops.vsm's Ax_HardwareCeiling: a study document that cites and
      extends canon, doesn't fork it.",

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // SECTION 1 — ROOT CAUSE OF THE RECOGNITION FAILURE (verified, not guessed)
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  section_1_root_cause: {

    headline: "This was NOT 'no instrumentation exists'. A real detector
        fired a CORRECT, independent algedonic alert within 2 minutes of the
        actual failure onset. It was defeated by two separate, precise,
        fixable bugs -- not a missing capability.",

    timeline_verified: [
      "2026-08-01 23:37:29 -- last confirmed-healthy clima-bridge activity before the incident (journalctl -u vsf-s4-clima-bridge, LXC 101)",
      "2026-08-01 23:45:01 -- service_watchdog.py (cron */5min, n02 host, script /root/vsf-scripts/service_watchdog.py) runs its cycle",
      "2026-08-01 23:45:06 -- check_nats() correctly detects failure: 'nats 10.10.20.20:4222: FAIL(timed out)' -- WITHIN 2 MINUTES of true onset",
      "2026-08-01 23:45:06 -- code DOES independently publish: _publish_algedonic(7, 'service_down', 'NATS unreachable: timed out') -- a real, correct, severity-7 algedonic event WAS emitted",
      "2026-08-01 23:47:13 -- last bundle vsf-s4-clima-bridge ever received before the fix (confirms the alert fired essentially at the moment of failure, not late)",
      "2026-08-01 23:55:06 -- check_nats() fires FAIL a second time, alert republished",
      "2026-08-01 23:55:xx through 2026-08-02 19:55:xx (~20 hours) -- check_nats() reports OK continuously -- see bug B below, this is a FALSE all-clear, not a real recovery",
      "2026-08-02 20:00:06 onward -- check_nats() resumes reporting FAIL every cycle, correctly, through the fix",
      "2026-08-02 21:56:47 -- pct reboot 106 (targeted, per rmw3's explicit scope call over a full n02 reboot) -- NATS clean, clima-bridge connects on attempt 1",
    ],

    bug_A_alert_fatigue: {
      what: "service_watchdog.py's checks that shell out via _pct_exec() (used
          for check_service, check_disk, check_ollama, check_alerts_db --
          covering lxc101/vsf-nucleus-operational, lxc101/vsf-motor2,
          lxc101/ollama, lxc104/vsf-s5-algedonic, lxc104/vsf-bbs-bridge,
          lxc106/nats-server[the pct-based one, DIFFERENT from check_nats's
          raw-socket check], lxc107/vsf-s3-star, lxc109/vsf-view-host) have
          been failing with 'FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or
          directory: pct' on EVERY SINGLE RUN since the very day this
          watchdog was deployed -- first occurrence verified in
          /var/log/vsf/watchdog.log at 2026-06-12T13:35:01Z, i.e. minutes
          after deployment. Root cause not yet fixed: almost certainly a
          PATH problem in the cron environment (cron's minimal PATH often
          excludes /usr/sbin where pct lives; interactive shells have it via
          profile scripts the cron env doesn't source).",
      impact: "Every 5 minutes, for ~7 weeks (2026-06-12 to 2026-08-02, ~51
          days x 288 cycles/day approx 14,700+ occurrences), this generates a
          'WATCHDOG ALERT sev=9 service_down: SERVICE DOWN: [8 services all
          listed as down]' -- a maximal-severity, maximally-broad false
          alarm claiming the ENTIRE stack is down, continuously, for 7
          weeks, while the system ran fine the whole time. Textbook cry-wolf:
          any human or automated consumer of this channel would have long
          since learned this specific alert shape means nothing and
          filtered/ignored it -- so when the ONE real, correctly-scoped,
          correctly-timed sev=7 NATS alert landed in the SAME channel at
          23:45:06, it had zero chance of standing out.",
      fix_shape: "Mechanical, not architectural: find and fix the pct PATH
          resolution in service_watchdog.py's cron execution context (e.g.
          set PATH explicitly in the cron.d entry, or use an absolute path
          like /usr/sbin/pct in _pct_exec()). Verify with a real run, not
          just a code read -- this exact class of 'looks fixed, still broken
          in the actual cron environment' has bitten this project before
          (see feedback_pct_exec_stdout, feedback_pct_exec_lxc0_pseudo_node).",
    },

    bug_B_probe_depth_gap: {
      what: "check_nats() does: socket.create_connection((host,port),
          timeout=5) -> s.recv(64) -> close. This validates TCP-connect +
          that the NATS server sends its initial INFO banner (which any NATS
          server sends immediately on ANY new raw connection, unauthenticated,
          before CONNECT/auth). It does NOT validate: authentication with the
          real token, a successful CONNECT handshake, or SUB/PUB actually
          working. A NATS server that accepts new raw TCP connections and
          hands out INFO banners, but is unable to actually register/serve
          authenticated application clients (exactly the state 106 was
          verified to be in -- systemd's own D-Bus reported 'Transport
          endpoint is not connected', and ss showed the accept queue at
          Recv-Q 4097 > Send-Q/backlog 4096, i.e. listening but not truly
          accepting), can pass this shallow probe while every real client
          (clima-bridge, using the actual token, doing a full CONNECT) keeps
          failing.",
      impact: "This is why the ONE correct signal (23:45/23:55) went quiet
          for ~20 hours instead of continuing to fire: the probe's own
          success criterion was satisfiable even during deep degradation,
          producing a FALSE ALL-CLEAR that would have misled anyone who
          filtered past bug A and actually found the real 23:45 alert into
          concluding the problem had resolved itself.",
      fix_shape: "Deepen check_nats() to do a real authenticated connect +
          a lightweight PING/PONG or a SUB to a throwaway subject, using the
          same token clima-bridge uses (already in
          /etc/systemd/system/vsf-s4-clima-bridge.service.d/nats-auth.conf).
          A shallow probe is fine as a FIRST layer (cheap, fast) but must not
          be the ONLY layer for a service other components depend on for
          real traffic.",
    },

    meta_gap_not_yet_resolved: "monitor_reconcile.py (cron every 6h, checks
        'last_seen staleness for all instruments', per
        monitoring_instruments.vsm gap(monitor_reconcile) marked RESOLVED
        2026-06-16) apparently never caught bug A. This is worth
        understanding, not assumed: staleness-checking (is this instrument
        still producing timestamps) is a DIFFERENT axis from
        correctness-checking (is what it's producing actually true) --
        service_watchdog.py never went stale, it ran every 5 minutes like
        clockwork for 7 weeks, it just always produced the SAME WRONG
        answer. A staleness monitor cannot see this class of bug by
        construction, the same way topology_reconcile's existence-check
        cannot see a crash-looping-but-technically-running service (see
        section 2). NOT investigated further here -- flagged as an open
        question for the implementation plan (section 3) rather than dug
        into mid-spec.",

    bbs_api_point_fix: "Separately, not the same incident chain but
        discovered investigating LXC 106's degraded state: bbs-api (already
        documented DEPRECATED 2026-05-24 post-colapso) is still running and
        still being polled every ~30s by something at 10.10.20.1 for
        channel=vsf-nucleus / channel=vsf-alerts -- both consistently 404
        (the endpoints don't exist). Its connections accumulate open file
        descriptors without being cleanly closed; 'OSError: Too many open
        files' errors confirmed starting 2026-08-02 17:55:00, worsening
        through 21:35:11 (668 occurrences in one burst), right up to the
        reboot. Given 106's shared per-container fd budget, this plausibly
        contributed to (or fully caused) the systemd D-Bus break and NATS
        accept-queue saturation found today -- a slow leak from a service
        nobody remembered was still running. Fix: disable bbs-api (it's
        already deprecated, should not be running at all) and find+stop
        whatever orphaned poller is still hitting it every 30s.",
  },

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // SECTION 2 — THE GENERAL PATTERN: why local signals don't reach R^128/Ω
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  section_2_general_pattern: {

    rmw3_thesis: "The R^128 state-vector / Ω-governance geometry exists
        specifically so the system can be governed WITHOUT the human
        supervising every organ. Human-in-the-loop belongs at the POLICY
        level (A5's autonomy ceiling: double-loop corrections require human
        approval); it should not be the mechanism by which single-loop,
        already-authorized corrections get triggered, because that
        collapses into exactly the 'human supervises every organ' failure
        mode the R^128 mapping was built to avoid.",

    the_axis_confusion: "Everything currently checking system health
        operates on ONE axis: does the declared thing EXIST (topology_
        reconcile: state.db vs pct/systemctl reality; service_watchdog's
        pct-based checks, when they work: is the systemd unit present).
        NOTHING generically checks the orthogonal axis: is the existing
        thing BEHAVING correctly, continuously. systemd's own Restart=
        always + WatchdogSec mechanism actively HIDES failures on this axis
        -- a service crash-looping every 85s looks, at any single sampled
        instant, indistinguishable from a healthy one to any existence-only
        check. This was true before today's investigation (thread already
        opened via TASKS.vsm LOCAL-RESILIENCE-NOT-WIRED-TO-ALGEDONIC) and
        remains the core diagnosis; today's forensics (section 1) sharpen
        it further: EVEN WHEN a real liveness-axis check exists (check_nats
        does), it can still be defeated by (a) noise from other broken
        existence-axis checks sharing its channel, and (b) insufficient
        probe depth on its own liveness definition.",

    three_prior_instances_this_session: [
      "PSI (cpu.pressure/io.pressure) -- accumulates real kernel data, zero consumers (thermal_resource_governance thread 4)",
      "vsf-thermal-watchdog.py (shipped this session) -- explicitly does NOT feed Omega/S3/S4/S5 by design, journal+state-file only",
      "vsf-s4-clima-bridge -- crash-looped 925x/~22h; NRestarts/ActiveState never watched by anything generic",
    ],

    fourth_instance_sharper: "service_watchdog.py's NATS check IS a liveness
        check, and it DID fire correctly (section 1) -- proving the pattern
        isn't just 'nothing checks liveness', it's 'liveness checks exist in
        isolated pockets, are not defended against noise from their own
        sibling checks, and are not depth-audited against what actually
        matters to real consumers'. This is a more precise, more solvable
        problem than 'build a new governance layer from scratch'.",

    what_r128_connection_should_mean: "Not every telemetric signal needs to
        become a new R^128 dimension -- that question was already answered
        correctly for temperature (thermal_resource_governance thread 1):
        Ax_HardwareCeiling deliberately excludes it from Ω_VSF. The right
        generalization is a CLASSIFICATION, applied per signal:
          (a) Ω/R^128 dimension -- reserved, rare, core viability state.
          (b) S3 resource-bargain input -- physical/infra constraint feeding
              graduated allocation decisions (temperature's resolved home;
              disk/FD-headroom likely belongs here too).
          (c) Algedonic trigger, single-loop-authorized -- per A5/D4, a
              known-safe corrective action exists (e.g. 'restart a service
              that's been flapping past a threshold') and should fire
              WITHOUT waking a human, same as any other automatable
              single-loop correction already permitted in this project.
          (d) Algedonic trigger, double-loop -- no known-safe automatic
              response, or first occurrence of a new failure shape --
              escalates to a human per A5, same as today.
          (e) Passive/audit only -- S3* can query it on demand; no push
              needed (e.g. SMART sector counts, checked opportunistically).
        Today's clima-bridge/NATS incident is a textbook (c): 'restart a
        degraded service showing sustained flapping + a broken D-Bus/
        saturated queue' is exactly the shape of correction A5/D4 already
        calls single-loop/automatable. It took a human noticing, asking,
        Claude diagnosing, Claude asking permission, and Claude executing --
        FOUR unnecessary human-in-the-loop hops for something the project's
        OWN existing rules already say shouldn't need one.",
  },

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // SECTION 3 — IMPLEMENTATION PLAN (staged, per rmw3's own sequencing)
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  section_3_implementation_plan: {

    context: "rmw3's own staging for this whole arc: (1) essential
        containers only (101,104,106,107,108,109,110) -- DONE, executed
        2026-08-02 same session; (2) map + wire the nucleus completely
        BEFORE reintroducing any S1; (3) only then reintegrate S1s. This
        plan is stage-2 content: it does not touch S1 reintegration at all.",

    stage_2a_mechanical_fixes: [
      "Fix service_watchdog.py's pct PATH bug (bug_A) -- smallest, highest-leverage fix in this whole spec: stops 14,700+/7wk false sev=9 alarms, restores the channel's actual signal-to-noise ratio for every OTHER real check it runs, not just NATS.",
      "Deepen check_nats() to a real authenticated CONNECT + PING (bug_B) -- closes the false-all-clear blind spot.",
      "Disable bbs-api + find/stop its orphaned poller (bbs_api_point_fix).",
      "Verify all three with REAL runs post-fix, not code review alone -- per feedback_verify_build_tree / feedback_pct_exec_stdout precedent in this project.",
    ],

    stage_2b_close_the_meta_gap: [
      "Investigate why monitor_reconcile.py's staleness check didn't catch bug A (meta_gap_not_yet_resolved) -- determine if it needs a CORRECTNESS dimension (e.g. sample-and-compare an instrument's output against an independent ground truth, not just 'did it produce a timestamp recently') alongside its existing staleness dimension.",
      "This closes the SAME axis-confusion (existence vs behavior) one meta-level up: a monitor that's alive-but-wrong is invisible to a monitor that only checks aliveness.",
    ],

    stage_2c_generic_liveness_layer: [
      "Build the NRestarts/ActiveState scanner already scoped in TASKS.vsm LOCAL-RESILIENCE-NOT-WIRED-TO-ALGEDONIC -- generic, per-service, systemd-native, zero domain knowledge needed. This is the cheapest, highest-coverage NEW instrument this spec calls for (everything else in stage 2a/2b is fixing what already exists).",
      "Classify its output per section_2's (a)-(e) taxonomy -- for THIS signal type (a service flapping/failed past a threshold), the classification is (c): single-loop-authorized automatic restart, algedonic-logged for audit but NOT gated on human approval, per A5/D4's own existing rule.",
    ],

    stage_2d_the_r128_question_proper: [
      "Only after 2a-2c are real and verified: revisit which of the disconnected signals from thermal_resource_governance's hardware inventory (PSI, disk SMART trend, AC/battery, disk-space trend) belong in classification (b) [S3-bargain input] vs (c) [single-loop algedonic] vs (e) [passive/audit]. This is explicitly the SAME open question thermal_resource_governance thread 1 already raised for temperature, generalized -- not a new design, a application of an already-agreed method to the rest of the inventory.",
      "This stage is where 'connect parameters to R^128' gets literal for the signals that actually belong there (classification b) -- expected to be a SMALL subset of the total; most of what's disconnected today turns out to belong in (c) or (e), not (a)/(b), per the pattern already established for temperature.",
    ],

    explicitly_out_of_scope_here: "Domain-specific liveness signals (e.g.
        clima-bridge's own 'last bundle received' timestamp, VD scan
        completion, agent keepalive freshness) are real and valuable but
        need per-service instrumentation -- correctly deferred past this
        spec's generic-layer scope (section_2's fourth_instance_sharper
        already names this distinction). Not blocking stage 2a-2d.",

    sequencing_note: "No urgency flag needed for 2b-2d beyond 'next open
        work window' (same standing as thermal_resource_governance) -- but
        2a should be treated as near-term given it is cheap, mechanical, and
        actively degrading the project's ability to trust its own algedonic
        channel RIGHT NOW, independent of anything else in this spec.",
  },
}
